Gave the user-menu components the stories they were missing. `IdentitiesMenuItem` had one; `UserAvatar`, `ProfileMenuItem` and the shadowed `PersonalTools` were added without any, so the only way to look at the toolbar avatar was to start the whole stack.

The avatar's stories are the states worth seeing side by side: a portrait, initials, five people at once — which is the only way to see the point of deriving the colour from the userid — a middle name that must not push the surname out, a single name, an anonymous visitor, and a portrait URL that fails to load. The menu ones show the "My profile" entry present and absent, which is the difference between a site running the `[profile]` layer and one that is not.

`PersonalTools` gets stories too, since it is this package's code now rather than Volto's: they are where this add-on's two plugged entries are seen *in place*, alongside each other, rather than each alone. A shared `withUser` decorator and a `USER` fixture went into the story fixtures, and the portrait is a data URI so a story renders the same with or without a network. `UserProfile` gained the `username` field the endpoint has always returned. @ericof
